{
  "$schema": "internal",
  "_meta": {
    "version": "1.1",
    "description": "Event persistence overrides (OB-176). Events not listed fall back to level-based default (warn+ persist, info suppressed). See EVENT_REGISTRY.md for the canonical event vocabulary.",
    "locked_at": "2026-05-26",
    "ruled_by": "Builder Q7-Q10 ruling 2026-05-25",
    "amendments": [
      "2026-05-27: Operator-action visibility pass. Added all admin.* + operator.* mutation events to overrides so Builder can see plan-mapping saves, API-key rotations, location/client lifecycle, and sub-member workflow in the trace timeline. Closes the OB-176 default-suppress operator-visibility gap surfaced in Builder feedback 2026-05-27.",
      "2026-07-09: DR-052 member-email visibility. Added the three info-level email.member.* events (sent/suppressed/skipped_disabled) so branded member-email send attempts show up in the trace timeline — without this override they default-suppress like any other info event (email.member.failed is warn-level and already persists by default). Found while diagnosing a silent test-send failure.",
      "2026-07-25: Operator-alert email redesign. Added email.operator.sent (the shared send path all six operator alerts now route through, via core/operator-mailer.js) and health.alert_suppressed (fires when the escalate-then-cool-down rule holds back a repeat hardware-key alert). Both are info-level, so without these overrides the Builder could not see which alerts were sent versus deliberately withheld. email.operator.failed is error-level and already persists by default.",
      "2026-08-04 (DR-054): Unregistered-event sweep. Audited all 28 info-level events emitted by production code that had no registry entry. The defect was not that they were suppressed — it was that an absent entry cannot distinguish 'deliberately noise' from 'nobody ever decided', so a load-bearing event could go missing without anyone noticing (which is exactly how DR-050's revoke.billing_cancelled was lost). Every one of the 28 now has an explicit entry: 12 promoted to persist because they answer 'why did or didn't this member get through the door', and 16 pinned to persist:false because they are per-job progress breadcrumbs. The persist:false entries are load-bearing in their own right — they silence the [logger] unregistered warning AND record the decision. A P3 test now fails if any production event name is missing from this file."
    ]
  },
